LIMITATIONS (read before quoting any number)
  - No timing in these fixtures was checked by hand by us. LibriSpeech/MFA timings are forced alignments with an
    HMM-GMM model (an algorithm independent of the CTC aligner under test); published MFA benchmarks put most word
    boundaries within a few tens of ms of hand labels, so the reference carries an error of that order (not measured
    by us). LibriSpeech is read audiobook speech, cleaner and slower than social-video speech.
  - The non-English references were produced by the authors with the Meta MMS forced aligner, which is a wav2vec2 CTC
    model like ours: errors common to CTC aligners (for example word ends placed early) are invisible against this
    reference, so the non-English numbers are optimistic. They were filtered to high-confidence alignments (mean word
    score > 0.9), and about 20% were checked by hand by the dataset authors, not by us.
  - No openly licensed, hand-verified word-timing set was found for Hindi or other Indic languages, Arabic, CJK or
    Thai (TIMIT and Buckeye are English-only and not free); those languages are not measured.
  - 10 clips, 220 reference words: enough to see whether the target is met by a wide margin, not to separate
    systems that are within ~10 ms of each other.
